<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- TemporalToggles — the PastCast / NowCast / ForeCast features as three SPLIT keys: a mode toggle with
     that mode's settings-window latch down its right edge.

       ┌──────────┬─┐┌──────────┬─┐┌──────────┬─┐   ┐ the MODE HALF is the bar's shared square
       │    ↺     │·││   ((•))  │·││    ☁     │·│   │ (BarKeySide), so its glyph and name match the
       │ PastCast │·││ NowCast  │·││ ForeCast │·│   │ Sites and Settings keys exactly; the car is
       │          │·││          │·││          │·│   │ added beside it, not carved out of it
       └──────────┴─┘└──────────┴─┘└──────────┴─┘   ┘ the SIDE CAR — one window per mode, latched here
            ^              ^              ^
            │              │              └── SPC outlooks
            │              └── live radar / current alerts
            └── historical replay

     ⚠️ EACH KEY OWNS ITS OWN WINDOW AGAIN, and that is the point of the split. The square is a MODE switch
     (lit = this mode is on the map, click a lit one to turn it off); the car is a WINDOW latch (filled =
     this mode's panel is on the screen). Two contracts, two controls, both visible at once — see
     Controls/Primitives/SplitTemporalToggle for the shape and the full history.
     HISTORY, do not undo: these keys ONCE owned a window each with no second control, so a click turned the
     mode on when unlit and flipped the window when lit — one click doing two different things depending on
     invisible state. That was fixed by moving all three panels into ONE tabbed Timeframe window behind a
     shared three-dot key at the end of this row. The car fixes the same bug without that cost: the panels
     are three windows again, so NowCast and ForeCast (which coexist) can be parked on two monitors.
     ⚠️ THE THREE-DOT KEY IS GONE with the tabbed window it opened. Do not re-add a shared panel key beside
     these three — there is no shared panel any more, and a fourth key in this row was exactly the thing that
     had to be kept nameless and half-width to stop it reading as a fourth mode.

     A MODE key is a GLYPH OVER A NAME and nothing else — it is a mode switch, not a readout. A third tier
     carrying live status (replay date / warning counts / outlook day) was built and REMOVED: it made the
     keys read as dashboard tiles when their whole job is "which timeframe am I in". Don't re-add it; that
     state belongs in the mode's own window.

     ⚠️ The glyphs are a REVERSAL of the older "text pills, not icon-over-label stacks" rule, made
     deliberately: that rule came from a SMALL pill, where it was icon OR word and the word won. A key this
     size fits both. Codepoints were picked by rendering candidates in the actual font — a wrong one ships as
     an empty box, so verify visually before swapping one. (The car's three-dot mark is DRAWN, not a glyph,
     precisely to keep that risk off it — and it is the mark the retired shared panel key used, which is why
     it needs no name to be read as "there is more behind this".)

     THE LOOK IS NOT HERE — it is SplitTemporalToggle, which owns both halves' states locally. ⚠️ These are
     the one cluster NOT on Controls/Styles.xaml's BarKeyToggleStyle: a key divided in two cannot use a style
     whose job is drawing ONE stroke around ONE square. The shared vocabulary is kept by hand there instead
     (same surfaces, same accent rule, and the same BarKeySide square from that file's SIZES section), so a
     change to the bar's key look now has TWO places to visit — the price of the split, and the reason both
     files say so. This file owns only WHICH three keys there are, what they say, and what they bind to.

     The keys carry the FULL names (PastCast / NowCast / ForeCast) so the display vocabulary matches the one
     the code uses everywhere. They sit at the bottom bar's exact HORIZONTAL CENTER, pinned there by
     MainWindow's [*, Auto, *] bar grid: this control is the Auto column between two equal stars, so neither
     the console on the left nor the window buttons on the right can push it off centre, however wide they
     get. With the three-dot key gone the row is three keys again, so the bar's midpoint falls on the middle
     of NowCast rather than between keys.
     ⚠️ Keep this control's own WIDTH content-sized and symmetric — a horizontal stretch or a one-sided
     margin here would re-introduce the drift the bar grid exists to prevent.

     SIZING — there is NONE here, and none in the code-behind. Every dimension is declared in XAML: the
     shared numbers in Controls/Styles.xaml's SIZES section (BarKeySide, the glyph and name sizes, the car's
     width), the per-key ones in the key that uses them. ⚠️ This is a reversal — the keys used to be MEASURED
     off the bar's content height by a whole helper class, because the per-pane product chips lived in the
     bar and grew it in quad layout. Those chips moved to the pane notches, the bar's height stopped moving,
     and the machinery was reacting to nothing. The keys declare their size and the bar's Auto row sizes to
     them. Read the ⚠️ history in that section before putting any of it back.
     Bound to the coordinator MapViewModel. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Composites.TemporalToggles"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:prim="using:Anvil.Controls.Primitives"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- ⚠️ Center, NOT Stretch — and this is a REVERSAL. The keys used to be measured against the height
         this row was given, so it had to fill the bar's content row or the squares collapsed. They declare
         their own size now (Styles.xaml, BarKeySide) and the bar's row sizes to THEM, so stretching this
         would only leave the keys floating in a taller row. -->
    <Grid x:Name="PillRow"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          ColumnSpacing="8">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!-- PASTCAST — glyph E81C, a clock with a counter-clockwise arrow: history/rewind. -->
        <prim:SplitTemporalToggle x:Name="PastKey"
                                  Grid.Column="0"
                                  Glyph="&#xE81C;"
                                  ModeName="PastCast"
                                  ModeToolTip="PastCast — replay a historical radar window (click again to turn it off)"
                                  IsModeOn="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsPastCast, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  IsPanelOpen="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsPastWindowOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ModeClick="OnPastClick"/>

        <!-- NOWCAST — glyph E93E, a dot radiating signal waves: live. Deliberately NOT the tower (EC05),
             which reads as "a radar site" and would collide with the on-map site markers' language. -->
        <prim:SplitTemporalToggle x:Name="NowKey"
                                  Grid.Column="1"
                                  Glyph="&#xE93E;"
                                  ModeName="NowCast"
                                  ModeToolTip="NowCast — live radar / current conditions (click again to turn it off)"
                                  IsModeOn="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsNowCast, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  IsPanelOpen="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsNowWindowOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ModeClick="OnNowClick"/>

        <!-- FORECAST — glyph E753, a cloud: the weather-outlook idiom. -->
        <prim:SplitTemporalToggle x:Name="ForeKey"
                                  Grid.Column="2"
                                  Glyph="&#xE753;"
                                  ModeName="ForeCast"
                                  ModeToolTip="ForeCast — SPC outlooks (click again to turn it off)"
                                  IsModeOn="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsForeCast, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  IsPanelOpen="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsForeWindowOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ModeClick="OnForeClick"/>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
